CHARACTER=A cheerful young beaver carpenter: warm chestnut-brown fur, a cream muzzle with two big white front teeth showing under a dark-brown nose, small round ears, and a broad flat dark-brown paddle tail. A turquoise knitted long-sleeved sweater with a ribbed collar and ribbed cuffs, mustard-yellow trousers held by a brown belt with a gold buckle, and red boots. Dark-brown mitten paws.
ROLE_HURDLE=image_2 = THE HURDLE, a prop already painted for this game: two chunky wooden posts on round feet with one thick red-and-white striped bar between them. Paint this exact hurdle, same colours and shapes; its size next to the character is given below.
ROLE_FLAG=image_2 = THE FLAG, a prop already painted for this game: a white cloth flag on a thick wooden stick. Paint this exact flag, same colours and shapes; its size next to the character is given below.
STORY_PLANT=In a sunny garden the beaver is planting one seed. On the ground just to the picture's right of its boots there is a small low mound of soft dark chocolate-brown soil, about as wide as one boot, close beside the right-hand boot. The beaver is not exercising: it bends down gently and carefully, the way a gardener does, to press the seed into the top of the mound with its paw, looking at it with quiet care.
STORY_HURDLE=The beaver is jumping over a small hurdle, like a child playing at a sports day. The hurdle stands on the ground right in front of its legs, about as tall as the boots plus the rolled-up trouser cuffs, and a little wider than the trousers. The beaver crouches, springs straight up and over it toward the viewer, and lands on the near side, light and bouncy, full of joy. The hurdle never moves.
STORY_FLAG=The beaver is cheering at a match, waving a white flag in big happy sweeps from side to side above its head. The flag is held by the bottom of its stick in the beaver's left paw, the paw on the picture's right. The stick is about as long as the beaver's whole body below the neck, the cloth about as big as the beaver's head.
PLACE_GROUND=The boots stand exactly where they stand in image_1, flat on the same ground line, and everything above them is placed from there.
PLACE_AIR=The ground line is the same as in image_1. If the whole pose does not fit at image_1's scale, zoom out just enough that everything fits inside the frame with a small margin, and keep the character centred.
POSE_PLANT_MID=Halfway down. Both knees have started to bend out toward the picture's left and right, and the hips are lowered by about an eighth of the character's height, the trousers creasing softly at the knees. The torso stays upright and square to the viewer. The face still looks out at the front as in image_1, tipped down a little, and only the eyes glance down at the mound, with a gentle closed smile. The paw on the picture's right holds one tiny golden-tan seed pinched between its finger and thumb and moves down and out toward the mound; the other paw is out to the picture's left for balance, a little away from the body. The tail rests on the ground behind.
POSE_PLANT_LOW=All the way down, a deep and comfortable gardener's crouch. Both knees are bent and spread wide apart, pointing out to the picture's left and right, and the trousers bunch into soft round folds at the knees. Both boots stay flat on the ground, a little wider apart than in image_1. HOW LOW: measured from the boot soles, the belt is now only half as high as it is in image_1, at about the height where the knees are in image_1. The sweater, the arms and the head all move down together with the belt by that same amount, so there is a wide band of empty background above the ears that was not there in image_1. The character is not painted smaller: head, sweater, paws and boots keep exactly image_1's size; only the legs fold. The thighs run almost level, out to the picture's left and right from the hips to the knees, and the shins come straight down from the knees into the boots, so each leg makes a wide bent corner. The torso stays upright and square to the viewer, the belt buckle in the middle between the knees. The arm on the picture's right reaches down and out to the mound, the paw pressing the seed into its top with the fingertips. The other paw rests on the knee on the picture's left. The face still looks out at the front as in image_1, tipped a little toward the picture's right, and only the eyes glance down at the mound, with a calm, happy, concentrated smile. The tail lies flat on the ground behind.
POSE_HURDLE_CROUCH=The moment before take-off, a deep spring-loaded crouch behind the hurdle, the striped bar crossing in front of the shins. Both knees are bent and spread wide apart, out to the picture's left and right, both boots flat on the ground. HOW LOW: measured from the boot soles, the belt is now only half as high as it is in image_1, at about the height where the knees are in image_1. The sweater, the arms and the head all move down together with the belt by that same amount, so there is a wide band of empty background above the ears that was not there in image_1. The character is not painted smaller: head, sweater, paws and boots keep exactly image_1's size; only the legs fold. The thighs run almost level, out to the picture's left and right from the hips to the knees, and the shins come straight down from the knees into the boots, so each leg makes a wide bent corner. Both arms swing down straight beside the body, the open paws low at the sides of the knees, ready to be thrown up. The torso is upright and square to the viewer. The head is up and the eyes look over the bar at the viewer, with a determined, excited little smile.
POSE_HURDLE_AIR=The top of the jump, the beaver in mid-air above the hurdle. Both boots are tucked up high, the soles just above the striped bar, the knees bent and drawn up toward the belly and spread out to the picture's left and right, so the legs make a wide diamond shape. The hurdle stands where it stood, on the ground, below the boots, with background visible between the boots and the bar. Both arms are flung up and out to the sides above the shoulders, paws open with the fingers spread. The tail swings up behind. The mouth is wide open in a delighted shout and the eyes are wide.
POSE_HURDLE_LAND=The landing, on the near side of the hurdle: both boots have just touched the ground, flat, a little wider apart than in image_1, and the hurdle now stands behind the legs, its striped bar showing behind the knees. The knees bend deeply and spread wide apart, out to the picture's left and right, to soak up the landing, the trousers bunching at the knees. HOW LOW: measured from the boot soles, the belt is now only half as high as it is in image_1, at about the height where the knees are in image_1. The sweater, the arms and the head all move down together with the belt by that same amount, so there is a wide band of empty background above the ears that was not there in image_1. The character is not painted smaller: head, sweater, paws and boots keep exactly image_1's size; only the legs fold. The thighs run almost level, out to the picture's left and right from the hips to the knees, and the shins come straight down from the knees into the boots, so each leg makes a wide bent corner. Both arms are out to the sides at about shoulder height for balance, paws open. The tail bounces down to the ground behind. A big happy laugh with the mouth open.
POSE_FLAG_OUT=One end of the sweep. The arm on the picture's right is raised high above the shoulder, the paw closed in a fist round the bottom of the stick, and the stick leans out toward the picture's right. The cloth streams out from the top of the stick toward the picture's right in big soft waves, as if caught by the swing. The other arm is relaxed, the paw resting on the hip on the picture's left. The body leans a little toward the flag, both boots flat on the ground. The head tilts up toward the flag, and the mouth is open in a happy cheer.
POSE_FLAG_IN=The other end of the sweep. The arm on the picture's right is still raised above the shoulder, the fist round the bottom of the stick, but now the stick leans over toward the picture's left, its top above the beaver's head, never in front of the face. The cloth trails behind it toward the picture's right, curling in big soft waves, as it swings back across. The other paw rests on the hip on the picture's left. The body leans a little toward the picture's left, both boots flat on the ground. The head tilts up to follow the flag, and the mouth is open in a happy cheer.
CHECK_PLANT=Knees bent out to the sides, boots flat in image_1's place, one paw at the soil mound on the picture's right.
CHECK_HURDLE=The hurdle from image_2 on the ground in front of the legs, the pose as described.
CHECK_FLAG=The flag from image_2 in the paw on the picture's right, held at the bottom of its stick, swung as described.
ROLE_SOIL=image_2 = THE SOIL MOUND, a prop already painted for this game: a low mound of dark-brown soil with a seed on top. Paint this exact mound, same colours and shapes; its size next to the character is given below.
ROLE_NONE=
STORY_SQUAT=The beaver is bending its knees to crouch down, gently and evenly, to look at something small on the ground right in front of its boots. This frame is the in-between drawing halfway down, between standing as in image_1 and a deep crouch.
POSE_HALF=Halfway down, symmetrical. Both knees are bent and spread a little apart, pointing out to the picture's left and right, the trousers creasing softly at the knees, both boots flat on the ground exactly where they are in image_1. HOW LOW: measured from the boot soles, the belt is now three quarters as high as it is in image_1. The sweater, the arms and the head all move down together with the belt by that same amount; the character is not painted smaller: head, sweater, paws and boots keep exactly image_1's size; only the legs bend. The torso is upright and square to the viewer. Both arms hang down and a little out from the sides, relaxed, paws open. The head looks out at the viewer with a gentle closed smile. The tail hangs to the side as in image_1.
CHECK_SQUAT=Knees bent out to the sides, belt three quarters as high as in image_1, boots flat in image_1's place, symmetrical.
MOVE_SQUAT=The movement: standing as in image_1, the knees bend out to the sides and the legs fold evenly down into the deep crouch of image_2.
STEPS_SQUAT=1. Standing, exactly the legs of image_1: straight legs, boots a little apart. 2. The knees just starting to bend and part. 3. Halfway: knees bent and spread out to the sides. 4. Knees bent wide, the thighs angled out and down. 5. The deep crouch of image_2: thighs almost level out to the sides, shins straight down into the boots. HOW TALL: measure each drawing from the top of its belt to the soles of its boots. Drawing 1 is as tall as image_1's legs; drawing 2 is nine tenths of that; drawing 3 four fifths; drawing 4 seven tenths; drawing 5 only three fifths. As every belt sits on the same line, the boots of the five drawings make a line that rises step by step from left to right, and the legs get shorter and wider as they fold, never only wider.
MOVE_JUMP=The movement: the legs push off the ground straight and stretched, then fold up under the belt into the tucked jump of image_2.
STEPS_JUMP=1. Standing, image_3 exactly: straight legs, boots flat. 2. Push-off: the same legs straightened and stretched just a little, the boots tipped down onto their toes, close together. 3. Just off the ground: the knees bending and lifting out to the sides, the boots hanging below them. 4. Almost tucked: knees high and wide, the boots raised toward the knees. 5. The full tuck of image_2: the knees drawn up high and spread wide in a diamond, the soles of the boots turned out to the sides just below the knees. HOW TALL: measure each drawing from the top of its belt to the lowest point of its boots. Drawing 1 is as tall as image_3; drawing 2 one and a tenth times that; drawing 3 nine tenths; drawing 4 three quarters; drawing 5 two thirds. The trousers stay as chunky as image_3's in every drawing: as wide, never longer and thinner.
REST_SQUAT=Drawing 1 is this image exactly, at the same size, with the same painting; every other drawing is painted exactly like it.
REST_JUMP=Drawing 1 is this image exactly, at the same size, with the same painting; every other drawing is painted exactly like it.
STEPS_SQUAT8=1. Standing, image_3 exactly. 2. The knees just starting to soften. 3. The knees bending a little and starting to part. 4. Knees bent and parting out to the sides. 5. Halfway down: knees bent and spread out to the sides. 6. Knees bent wide, the thighs angled out and down. 7. Nearly there: thighs almost level. 8. The deep crouch of image_2: thighs almost level out to the sides, shins straight down into the boots. HOW TALL: measure each drawing from the top of its belt to the soles of its boots, as a fraction of drawing 1: drawing 2 is 0.97, drawing 3 0.93, drawing 4 0.88, drawing 5 0.82, drawing 6 0.75, drawing 7 0.68, drawing 8 0.6. The legs get shorter and wider as they fold, never only wider; each step is the same small change.
STEPS_JUMP8=1. Standing, image_3 exactly: straight legs, boots flat. 2. Push-off: the same legs straightened and stretched a little, both boots tipped down onto their toes, close together. 3. Leaving the ground: the knees just starting to bend, the boots hanging straight down on their toes. 4. The knees bending and lifting a little out to the sides. 5. Knees bent and drawn up and out, the boots lifting. 6. Knees higher and wider, the boots raised toward the knees. 7. Almost tucked: knees high and wide in a diamond. 8. The full tuck of image_2: the knees drawn up high and spread wide in a diamond, the soles of the boots turned out to the sides just below the knees. HOW TALL: measure each drawing from the top of its belt to the lowest point of its boots, as a fraction of drawing 1: drawing 2 is 1.1, drawing 3 1.0, drawing 4 0.92, drawing 5 0.84, drawing 6 0.77, drawing 7 0.71, drawing 8 0.66. The trousers stay as chunky as image_3's in every drawing: as wide, never longer and thinner; each step is the same small change.
